      "writtenOn": "2024-10-31T21:16:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Right, but calling Exit::Destroy() removes the exit from the Switch\u0027s list of Exits, which invalidates the iterator. The workaround I\u0027ve seen thus far is to iterate over a Vector copy of the Exits (over `swtch-\u003eExits().Vector()`), which works, but it\u0027s unfortunate that we have to make a copy. Ideally, we\u0027d find a way to remove the element from the container, and get the next valid iterator, like how [C++ `erase`](https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container/unordered_set/erase).\n\nI\u0027ll do the Vector() thing for now, unless a better way is suggested.",
      "parentUuid": "9469c66d_188cb2e1",
      "revId": "7c3b14104f48ccf234353763cead072a03e28a8b",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
